    "['set protocols ospf area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper OSPF interface configuration to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The OSPF process ID is arbitrary in Cisco (I used 1)\\n2. The Cisco wildcard mask is used (inverse of subnet mask)\\n3. The network statements cover all interfaces mentioned (lo0, ge-0/0/0, ge-0/0/1) using their respective IP ranges\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper OSPF interface configuration to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The OSPF process ID is arbitrary in Cisco (I used 1)\\n2. The Cisco wildcard mask is used (inverse of subnet mask)\\n3. The network statements cover all interfaces mentioned (lo0, ge-0/0/0, ge-0/0/1) using their respective IP ranges\", 'trans_template': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper OSPF interface configuration to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The OSPF process ID is arbitrary in Cisco (I used 1)\\n2. The Cisco wildcard mask is used (inverse of subnet mask)\\n3. The network statements cover