    "['interface [interface_name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF requires a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The loopback address 10.35.5.9 is explicitly configured with a wildcard mask of 0.0.0.0 to match exactly this address\\n3. The area is specified as 0 (equivalent to Juniper's 0.0.0.0)\\n4. Unlike Juniper where interfaces are specified directly, Cisco uses network statements with wildcard masks\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the Juniper OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF requires a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The loopback address 10.35.5.9 is explicitly configured with a wildcard mask of 0.0.0.0 to match exactly this address\\n3. The area is specified as 0 (equivalent to Juniper's 0.0.0.0)\\n4. Unlike Juniper where interfaces are specified directly, Cisco uses network statements with wildcard masks\", 'trans_template': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0 area 0'}]]": 1,
